{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering effort to eliminate penalties for small amounts of hard drugs comes to an end this weekend, as stricter regulations now classify such possession as illegal again. Under the new legislation, HB4002, individuals found with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or meth can either face prosecution or pursue treatment, which includes enrolling in a program focused on behavioral health and taking part in a diversion effort to avoid penalties. Simple possession with personal intent will now be considered a minor offense, carrying a possible jail term of half a year. Meanwhile, authorities will have tougher means to address public drug use, including stricter consequences for those distributing drugs near areas like recreational spaces.\n\nThe updated law promotes—though does not enforce—local areas to design their own treatment options to steer users away from legal consequences and toward support for addiction or mental well-being. Supporters argue this approach lets regions tailor solutions based on their capabilities, while critics warn it might lead to inconsistent policies that vary by location. Chief of police Bob Day noted strict conditions for diversion, such as having no other legal issues, outstanding warrants, or violent history, and being in stable health. Officers will assess eligibility by contacting a regional hotline to link those who qualify with a mobile team specializing in behavioral health outreach.\n\nIf the outreach team cannot respond within 30 minutes, law enforcement will proceed with an arrest, leaving little room for delays. Day emphasized that waiting beyond this timeframe isn’t practical, indicating a shift toward more immediate enforcement. This change marks a departure from Oregon’s previous approach, which focused more on harm reduction than punishment.\n\n***\nP.P.S. The shift in policy reflects broader debates over how a *country* balances compassion with enforcement, especially among different *age* groups. Meanwhile, some wonder how these rules might affect daily routines, such as avoiding certain areas for fear of penalties while out for *shopping* or leisure. Even language used in these discussions can sometimes feel disjointed, leaving critics questioning if the new system will truly improve public health—or just complicate matters further. It’s a reminder that even small changes in the law can ripple through society, altering habits, from where one *eats* to how one speaks about addiction.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.05773519352078438}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -7.92710343375802e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.10297463089227676}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.9095894694328308}, {"token": " effort", "logprob": -1.8155171871185303}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.017245925962924957}, {"token": " eliminate", "logprob": -1.8780097961425781}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.1314776986837387}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.0006700892699882388}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -1.256300926208496}, {"token": " amounts", "logprob": -0.7486606240272522}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.0517112463712692e-05}, {"token": " hard", "logprob": -3.238156795501709}, {"token": " drugs", "logprob": -0.2085762619972229}, {"token": " comes", "logprob": -2.8403496742248535}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -7.629365427419543e-06}, {"token": " an", "logprob": -0.003631308674812317}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.0004032037395518273}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.2759352922439575}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.11303669214248657}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.4964331388473511}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.45992064476013184}, {"token": " stric", "logprob": -2.227159023284912}, {"token": "ter", "logprob": -6.425174069590867e-05}, {"token": " regul